   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the state finance law, in relation to five-year capital plans for the state university of New York and the city university of New York",
    "basePrintNo": "S1625",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S1625-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 48,
       "fullName": "Patty Ritchie",
       "imgName": "423_patty_ritchie.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 423,
       "sessionMemberId": 949,
       "sessionYear": 2017,
       "shortName": "RITCHIE"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §22-c, St Fin L",
    "lawSection": "State Finance Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S1625\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the state finance law, in relation to five-year\ncapital plans for the state university of New York and the city\nuniversity of New York\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo provide five year capital plans for SUNY and CUNY for the fiscal\nyear beginning on April 1, 2018.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1. Amends section 22-c of the state finance law to direct the\nGovernor to submit a five-year capital plan that meets 100 percent of\nthe critical maintenance needs identified by each public university\nsystem or annually reduces the facility condition index for each\nuniversity system.\n\nSection 2. Provides for an immediate effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nAs part of the fiscal year 2008-2009 budget, the Legislature enacted a\nfive-year capital plan with annual critical maintenance appropriations\nof $550 million for the State University of New York and $284.2\nmillion for the City University of New York. These appropriations\nprovided the State's public colleges and universities with the ability\nto plan for capital expenditures and substantially reduce the backlog\nof critical maintenance projects. Since the conclusion of that plan in\nthe 2012-2013 budget cycle, the budget process has failed to produce a\nsimilarly robust capital plan for the SUNY and CUNY systems. As such,\nthe academic facilities at our public colleges and universities, as\nwell as the three SUNY hospitals, are in danger of not receiving the\nannual investment necessary for proper maintenance.\n\nThis bill would incorporate capital planning for SUNY and CUNY into\nthe overall capital plan submitted to the Legislature by requiring\nthat the Governor to submit five year capital plans for SUNY and CUNY\nas part of the Executive Budget. This requirement would ensure that\nthe facilities at our public institutions of higher education receive\nthe capital funding necessary to meet their annual critical\nmaintenance needs or reduce the facility condition index which\nmeasures the current condition of facilities requiring capital\ninvestment.\n\nAlthough this bill was vetoed by the Governor in 2015, this measure is\nstill of the utmost importance. Ensuring capital funding for SUNY and\nCUNY is essential to their ability to continue to provide exemplary\neducation for students of the State of New York. Building\ninfrastructure across SUNY and CUNY campuses remains in dire need of\ncritical maintenance because buildings are crumbling under the\nstudents' feet. This funding will further assist the efforts to reduce\nefforts to reduce facility condition indexes.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2015 S.5217/A.7388\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nTo be determined.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
